{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n64 \n \n \nWeek 17: June 28 \u2013 July 4 \nOn GMT 179, Christina Koch replaced the CIR Manifold #2 Bottle to continue supporting ACME Flame \nDesign test points. \n \nOn GMT 182, 15 spherical flames were successfully ignited for ACME Flame Design. The goal of Flame \nDesign is to improve our understanding of soot processes so that flames can be \u201cdesigned\u201d to be soot \nfree. \n \nWeek 18: July 5-11 \nOn GMT 189, ACME Flame Design test points were performed using diluted ethylene fuel at a chamber \npressure of 1.3 atm with varying oxygen concentrations of 34 to 40% oxygen (the remainder was \nnitrogen). The elevated oxygen and pressure can enhance the production of soot, where this research is \nfocused on understanding the conditions that lead to sooty and soot-free flames. \n \nOn GMT 192, Christina Koch replaced the ACME Bypass Controller and the CIR Manifold #2 Bottle to \nsupport Flame Design test points with slower nitrogen flow rates and in oxygen concentrations of up to \n40%. \n \nCAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG \nThis test point video shows a sooty flame, and, in the absence of buoyancy, the soot stays within \nthe flame and clusters. Some of the glowing soot clusters move out of the flame due to the \noutward flow of the diluted ethylene fuel through the burner\u2019s porous sphere. In contrast, some \nof the clusters get trapped within the flame due to a temperature gradient that moves them \ninward toward the cold burner and away from the hot flame. \n \nAfter some time, the harp-like array of 14-micron ceramic fibers is translated into the flame to \ndetermine the radial temperature distribution. Using a carefully characterized camera, the \ntemperature can be quantitatively determined from a glowing fiber\u2019s color, where yellow is hotter \nthan orange, and orange is hotter than red. The motion of the fiber arm causes the soot clusters \nto move away from the fibers.  \n \nWhile the temperature measurements are being made, the flow of diluted ethylene is gradually \nreduced, which causes the flame to become smaller and unstable. Heat loss causes the flame to \nlocally quench\u2014creating a hole in the flame near the burner tube. This hole allows for increased \nmixing of the fuel and oxygen, which reinvigorates the flame and causes it to close again. The \nflame opens and closes, effectively consuming fuel in repeated gulps\u2026a bit like a video-game", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 64}}